        <div id="who-should-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Should Not Cold Plunge, and the Risks for Everyone</h2>
<p>
            Start here. Cold water is not a gentle stimulus, and the manuals for cold tubs are more
            emphatic than most fitness equipment manuals. We read two in full — the instruction manual for
            an Ice Plunge Tub sold through Canadian Tire, and SaunaSpa's user guide for its CP-CH6732N
            chilled cold plunge — along with the guidance on Ice Barrel's and Plunge's own sites.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Heart and blood pressure.</strong> The Ice Plunge manual says to consult your doctor
              if you have heart disease, high blood pressure, respiratory problems or diabetes, or are
              pregnant, and that "sudden exposure to cold water can place stress on the heart. Individuals
              with cardiovascular conditions should use extreme caution and consider avoiding use."
              SaunaSpa's guide lists obesity, low or high blood pressure, circulatory problems, diabetes,
              heart disease, infectious diseases and immune deficiency as reasons to consult a doctor.</li>
            <li><strong>Pregnancy.</strong> Both manuals say to consult a doctor first.</li>
            <li><strong>Medication, alcohol and drugs.</strong> SaunaSpa says some medicines cause drowsiness
              or affect heart rate, blood pressure and circulation, and that using the plunge after alcohol,
              drugs or medication may result in unconsciousness and drowning. The Ice Plunge manual warns
              that alcohol and drugs "can greatly increase the risk of fatal hypothermia."</li>
            <li><strong>Raynaud's and circulation conditions.</strong> None of the manuals we read names
              Raynaud's phenomenon; their circulation warnings cover it. Garage Gym Reviews' how-to guide
              to cold plunging names Raynaud's, alongside high blood pressure, arrhythmia and
              other heart disease, as reasons to see a doctor before plunging. If cold already turns your
              fingers white or numb, ask before you buy.</li>
            <li><strong>Children.</strong> The Ice Plunge manual says children should not use the tub, and
              elsewhere that they should only ever do so under supervision; SaunaSpa says not to let children
              use the plunge unless accompanied by an adult and to secure the cover so children cannot get
              in.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>The risks that apply to everyone.</strong> The Ice Plunge manual lists them plainly:
            an <strong>involuntary gasp reflex and hyperventilation</strong> on entry, so get in slowly and
            control your breathing; <strong>dizziness and fainting</strong> from rapid temperature change;
            <strong>hypothermia</strong> with prolonged exposure, with signs including uncontrollable
            shivering, confusion, slurred speech and drowsiness; and <strong>frostbite</strong> risk in the
            fingers and toes. It says, "Always use the ice plunge tub under supervision. Do not use the tub
            alone in case of emergencies." That is the rule we would underline: never plunge alone, and
            never with your head under water.
          </p>
        </div>

